{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Speak to the children of Israel.</b> He was commanded to inform them of the matter of the burnt-offering in general. <b>And say to them.</b> In particular — to inform them of the specific procedures in the burnt-offering, namely that one skins and cuts it into pieces, and the matter of slaughter, that it must be in the north and not in the other directions; and likewise to inform them who may slaughter, and which woods are fit for the offering. And this is the meaning of <b>Speak to the children of Israel and say to them.</b> In every place in the Torah, the 'speaking' (דבור) concerns the general rule of the commandment, and the 'saying' (אמירה) concerns its particulars; and so too (Numbers 15) 'Speak to the children of Israel' regarding the commandment of tzitzit — to inform them first of the commandment of tzitzit in general, 'and say to them' in particular. And the particular is what comes by way of the received tradition (קבלה): that they set eight threads in each corner, making 32 on its four corners for a known intention, and likewise five knots — all this is included in what it says, 'and say to them,' for the particulars of the commandments cannot be elucidated from the verses at all." "<b>When any man of you brings.</b> From this language it would appear that it is possible to bring an offering from a human being, for it should have said 'a man of you, when he brings an offering'; but Scripture prevents and forbids it, and its meaning is: a man — when he wishes to bring from you an offering to HaShem — from the animals, from the cattle or from the flock, you shall bring it; not from a human being. And this is the prophet's saying (Jeremiah 19:5): ״אשר לא צויתי ולא דברתי ולא עלתה על לבי״. And because of this, the section of the offerings begins with the word אדם, to say that the man shall be the one bringing the offering and not the offering itself. And the precise grammarians said that the syntax of this verse is: אדם מכם כי יקריב — 'a man of you, when he brings.' And like it (Isaiah 26:11): ״אף אש צריך תאכלם״, its syntax is אף צריך אש תאכלם; and similarly (Hosea 8:2): ״לי יזעקו אלהי ידענוך ישראל״, its syntax is לי יזעקו ישראל אלהי ידענוך; and similarly (Exodus 22): ״אשר יאמר כי הוא זה״, its syntax is כי זה הוא; and many like these, by way of inversion." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'When any man (אדם) of you brings' — why is אדם said? Just as the first man (אדם הראשון) did not bring from stolen property, for everything was his, so too you shall not bring from stolen property; and thus it says (Isaiah 61:8): ״שונא גזל בעולה״." "<b>An offering to HaShem.</b> Every place where 'an offering to HaShem' is said, it says 'to HaShem' spelled with yod-heh, so as not to give an opening to the sectarians to argue. Scripture did not say 'to HaShem an offering,' but rather 'an offering to HaShem,' and from this our Rabbis of blessed memory derived: from where do we know that one should not say 'to HaShem a burnt-offering,' 'to HaShem a meal-offering,' 'to HaShem peace-offerings,' but rather 'a burnt-offering to HaShem,' 'a meal-offering to HaShem,' 'peace-offerings to HaShem'? Scripture says: 'an offering to HaShem.' And the matter is an a fortiori argument (קל וחומר): if regarding one who is about to bring an offering, the Torah said, 'My name shall not rest except upon the offering,' how much more so one who mentions the Name of Heaven for nothing." "<b>From the animals.</b> And not from the wild beasts. Scripture deemed fit for the purpose of offering the domesticated animals, which are pursued, and disqualified the wild beasts, which pursue. And from this our Rabbis of blessed memory derived: 'Be among the persecuted and not among the persecutors, among the insulted and not among the insulters, among the pursued and not among the pursuers' — for behold, you have no pursued creatures among birds like turtledoves and young pigeons, and Scripture deemed them fit for the altar. If so, whether among animals or among birds, the Torah deemed fit for the altar only the pursued. And this is what they said: 'Be among the insulted and not among the insulters, among the pursued and not among the pursuers.' They proved it, our Rabbis of blessed memory, from the verse that says (Judges 5:31): ״ואוהביו כצאת השמש בגבורתו״. And the meaning of the verse is that Scripture called the persecuted ones 'His beloved,' and likened them to the sun, because the sun was persecuted (עלוב) when the moon slandered it and said: 'It is impossible for two kings to use one crown.' And just as it likened their persecution to the persecution of the sun, so it likened their reward and the merit of the light of their soul to the merit of the light of the sun — ״כצאת השמש בגבורתו״." "<b>Further.</b> It may also be explained that Scripture qualified the domestic animals for the altar in order to indicate the quality of integrity (תמימות), by which it is fitting for a person to conduct himself. For the species of domestic animals is blameless (תם), and their dwelling is in settled areas, and in them our father Jacob chose, as his mother said to him (Genesis 27:9): ״לך נא אל הצאן״, and he would choose the way of peace and was called ״איש תם״, which the Targum renders ״גבר שלים״, and the Torah that was given to his seed is entirely peace, as it says (Proverbs 3:17): ״דרכיה דרכי נועם וכל נתיבותיה שלום״, and likewise Israel are called שולמית, as it says (Song of Songs 7:1): ״שובי שובי השולמית״, because they are a nation within whom the peace of the worlds dwells. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, chose this species for the offering, for the offerings are peace in the world. And He disqualified the wild beasts, because the wicked Esau, distant from the east of the sun in his might, was ״איש שעיר איש מדברי״, in the manner of what is said (Genesis 25:27): ״יודע ציד איש שדה״, and he chose that species of wild beasts whose dwelling is in the wilderness, a place of destruction and desolation — and behold, this is the opposite of the way of peace. And for this reason the Torah distances the hewn stone from the altar, because of the iron, which is the sword that destroys the world, and it is an inheritance to Esau; and this is what is written (Exodus 20:22): ״כי חרבך הנפת עליה ותחללה״, and since the altar prolongs days and the sword shortens them, our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded on this: it is only fitting that the one who shortens wield over the one who prolongs. And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: ״מן הבהמה מן הבקר ומן הצאן״ — ״מן הבהמה״ to exclude one that has lain with a male or been lain with; ״מן הבקר״ to exclude one that has been worshipped; ״מן הצאן״ to exclude one that has been set aside; and the extra ״ו״ to exclude the goring ox that has killed."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {4 "Comment [4] appears under the lemma מן הבהמה (Lev 1:2), but its content (animals vs. wild beasts, turtledoves) more directly addresses מן הבהמה as the general category in verse 2 before the verse narrows to cattle and flock; the midrashic proof-texts and reasoning span Lev 1:2 and 1:14 (birds)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:54.084401475Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}